WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
[Hank, voiceover]: Good morning, John! This week I woke up in New York City,

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:03.860
and my hair looked like this,

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:05.840
and then I went outside and it looked like this,

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:10.320
and then I went back inside and filmed an episode of The Financial Diet, which looked like this.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.280
"You can watch it at youtube.com/TheFinancialDiet."

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:16.040
And then I saw you, and you looked like this:

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:18.600
[John]: "It's a reunion video!"

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:19.700
[Hank]: "Hi." [John]: "Hey, how's it going?"

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.940
And then Sarah took us around to a bunch of amazing art galleries in Chelsea,

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.100
and they looked like this, and this, and this.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:28.520
And I felt very lucky that you are married to someone who understands art,

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.940
because even as a person who doesn't really think very much about art,

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:35.220
I still really appreciate it and it's wonderful to look at amazing art,

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:40.420
but it is difficult to find it – or if you do find it – to feel like you belong in those spaces,

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:44.260
with their giant white walls and terrifying price lists.

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:48.160
At one point I went into a Banana Republic and I got a shirt for the thing that we're doing tonight–

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.520
and I didn't take any footage of that, but I wanted to mention it

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:55.180
because of how much I appreciated the quiet assertiveness of the young man who helped me pick out my outfit

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.360
because I would've been totally lost without him.

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.900
When I'm in New York, I am, for some reason, more than anywhere else,

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.080
amazed by the reality that it all somehow works.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.320
That all of the people, from the cab drivers to the garbage collectors,

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.900
to the guy at the Banana Republic who helps fools like me look presentable,

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.240
to the people pruning and decorating the Christmas tree at Rockefeller Center,

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.800
all come together to make this thing function.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.160
I mean, New York City is full of amazing and beautiful things,

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.360
but in a lot of ways the most amazing thing I saw today was this:

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:27.500
Not because I have tremendous brand affinity for Pret a Manger,

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.280
which, to be clear, I totally do, but because food is complicated.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:35.000
So many ingredients edible for such a short time after their creation,

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.760
and yet there's always food. It might cost more than in the rest of the world, but it's always there.

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:45.160
And I'm not amazed at how good it is, or how good it tastes, but simply at the logistics–

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:48.920
how does it happen? How do we feed and water all these people?

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:52.400
Sometimes it seems like everything is broken, and nothing works at all.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.920
But when things work, we don't tend to notice.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:59.900
There's nothing quite like knowing, as I have my whole life, that there will be food at the corner store.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:04.200
I don't spend enough time being grateful for that, and not just grateful that I can always get food,

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.400
but grateful that so many people come together and work hard to make sure

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:14.480
that all of the people in New York City can continue eating and drinking, and everything can continue to function.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.500
That's not something that just happens. That's something that people do.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:20.760
John, I'll see you tonight.

